so when you know that you are  about to go back into a new school term  and it's going to be  via remote learning i mean some people  might say hey but everything you do  is online what's the problem i mean do  you really  notice i mean how do you feel at that  thought of going  back into remote learning yeah look i  mean  i do understand what people mean i've  had i guess a lot of practice you know  nine plus years of being able to speak  at a camera and record things and do all  the technology side of it  um but the answer is there is a huge  difference and i remember  um you know say 12 months ago as we kind  of came out of  the first lockdown here in new south  wales and when we returned back into the  classroom and i could see  the smiling faces of my students or not  even the smiling faces sometimes i'd  explain something  and i could read the confusion on their  face and i thought okay eddie you have  not explained that clearly you need to  do that better  even me looking at your face right now  there's a huge difference  to um from that to trying to deliver  learning to sometimes hundreds of  students at a time  and lacking the feedback and the  interaction which you know  we can do this through a chat but it  isn't the same is it and you know being  able to even  sit beside a student i remember teaching  some pre-service teachers people who  were training to be educators  and saying to them they're really  important things that you communicate to  your students  through your tone of voice through your  posture you know even when i remember as  an  early career teacher learning not to  stand over students  but to sit beside them or even kneel  beside them it's the smallest thing but  actually communicates here i am here to  support you  here to learn alongside you not to kind  of frown at your inability to do this  question  those kinds of things are so missed when  we go  on trim remote learning so even though  i'm grateful for how accessible  technology makes learning  like this i'm still craving the time to  be back with my students face  to face and i love that because what  you're just talking about there is what  it means to be human  and of course kindness is part of what  it means to be human  one more question about math eddie you  say it's a misconception that people  have a brain for maths  and the only thing i'd say to that is  really  your um your incredulity is not  surprising to me jane  i do get it though i mean the way i like  to think about it is that  uh this is a a very silly metaphor but  um i have i don't know how much exactly  you can see on the frame but  i have a fairly slender build i'm  never going to be really muscular or  strong  and so it's a fairly natural thing to  say it's like well okay  you're never going to be an athlete  right it's any  that's not what you're built for right  now i think most people  who are listening or watching in will  say yeah maybe  but you could certainly be fitter and  more athletic than you are now  if you did nothing if you just sat on  your butt for all of lockdown  and didn't get out and enjoy the sun as  it turns out  i actually really do enjoy a particular  kind of exercise i'm a long distance  runner  um turns out having a slender build is  really well fit for that  and i guess what my point is bring it  back to maths is that i know  we often might think okay i just was not  able to  crunch numbers really quickly or  memorize formulas  that's a very common experience and my  response to that is yes but that's only  a part  of mathematics i'm going to shift  metaphors for a second but  my three kids they're all very musical  and they love to sing and play the drums  and the piano and the flute  and i love listening to them most of the  time some of their practice  it clearly needs development but when i  think about them playing music  what i love about music is how broad and  diverse it is  uh my son who's a drummer part of the  reason he plays it is his arms are not  long enough  to play a flute which my daughter can i  just needed to find the part of music  that he could enjoy and that he could uh  really grasp  and i think the same is true of  mathematics many of us  believe that mathematics is not for us  because there's just a particular part  of it  that we didn't have a good time learning  at school but i truly believe  anyone can find their place in  mathematics it's like music we just need  to find the songs we enjoy  you are a technology as well as a  mathematics teacher and i wonder do you  ever think about  the kind of world your kids are going to  grow up in i mean this is such a huge  issue  at the moment as we contemplate  artificial intelligence and the  difference between machines and humans  you know  we have abstraction um but but do you  ever kind of  think and perhaps even worry about  a future where your kids will grow up  the short answer is yes i mean i'm not  sure if there are any parents  out there who haven't at least once  contemplated  wow you know if i grew up in the world  that my children are growing up in i  don't know  how i would relate how i would be able  to you know  look anyone in the eye knowing that my  entire life has been catalogued online  through facebook or twitter or snapchat  or whatever it happens to be  um and yeah i think about um yeah my  kids and particularly  social media which is kind of in many  ways what i've  hopefully used to really help a lot of  people um it also has a dark side  i think that every power that humanity  has ever come up with um you know power  is  not moral it depends on how it's used  whether it achieves good  or ill and so i do think with a little  bit of nervousness about the kind of  world my children are walking into  but i also know that this is not  new in a sense um the development of  technology throughout  history um is something which humanity  has always had to wrestle with  and you know it is never going to serve  us to just kind of shrink back and say  well i just don't have the um the time  or the thought or the effort  um or the wisdom to be able to  understand the situation we've got to  process that we've got to work that out  together and find a wise path through it  no matter what it looks like  now i'll just mention to everybody who's  on  this uh virtual conversation that we've  got about  three minutes maybe four minutes if i'm  a little greedy and i  keep wanting to ask any questions so i  hope you'll get ready  and think of questions that you would  like to ask eddie and please use the q  and a  tab for that so i don't have to switch  things around too much that's being  helpful to me  kind to me thank you eddie  you've said that you love being in the  classroom and your intent is to stay  there as long as possible  it's what animates you and gives you  energy  how do you know though that something  else couldn't make you  even happier uh that's a really good  question  uh i mean i guess the most direct way i  can answer that is to say  i don't know there are so many things in  my life which  if i had you know come back from now and  gone to eddie in the past and said hey  guess what this is going to happen i'd  say really  like how how can you i could have a  picture some of the things that have  happened to me  um in the last few years um i couldn't  even have contemplated so  i know enough about myself to know that  the future  holds things of uncertainty and i'm okay  to embrace that  um but i guess you know where is that  that quote that you just mentioned that  conviction  where that comes from is that you know  if there's this funny thing which i know  education  isn't the only uh you know industry that  does this  but we have this funny uh  counter-intuitive counterproductive  habit  of saying wow you are a great teacher  you are fantastic at working with  children in the classroom  i know what we'll do we'll promote you  right out of there and sit you in an  office or put you in some kind of role  of management where actually your skill  set is not that useful  and so me wanting to stay close to the  classroom close to students  is a recognition of the fact that i  think that is where  my core skills are but even like right  now i am not just a  teacher at technology high school i have  the great privilege of working with  the new south wales department of  education and working across schools  broadly  and i like to think and i guess the only  people who will be able to tell me  otherwise the people who i i work with  and who i get to interact with  i like to think that the work i do  outside the classroom is of a better and  a different quality  because i still remain in the classroom  i still have that currency  i still have that understanding of the  unique challenges and joys  of being in front of students wrestling  with curriculum changes as they roll  through that's what's in the news at the  moment  i feel that's a great privilege and i  never want to lose that
 

